---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ReleaseLifecycle from "../../components/diagrams/ReleaseLifecycle.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Policy Model"
  description="BeaconGuard Compliance Playbook hierarchy and control scopes for Request Admission, Result-to-Action Authorization, shared transaction controls, and evidence obligations."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>Policy Model</h1>

    <p>
      BeaconGuard control intent is expressed as a <strong>Compliance Playbook</strong>
      that is validated, compiled, approved, signed, and activated before runtime
      use. Only an <strong>Active Verified Release</strong> governs runtime
      evaluation at configured authorization points.
    </p>

    <p>
      The model is designed so that the same trusted inputs and the same Active
      Verified Release produce the same Deterministic Decision Result: <strong>ALLOW</strong>
      or <strong>DENY</strong>.
    </p>

    <ReleaseLifecycle />

    <h2>Release Hierarchy</h2>

    <div class="docs-table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Stage</th>
            <th>Role</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Compliance Playbook</td>
            <td>Authoritative control intent for a governed workflow</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Validation and Compilation</td>
            <td>Produces the deterministic compiled control representation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Signed Immutable Release Artifact</td>
            <td>Release package binding playbook identity and compiled controls</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Controlled Release Distribution</td>
            <td>Distributes the signed artifact to runtime environments</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Release Verification and Activation</td>
            <td>Verifies required release integrity and trust conditions before activation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Active Verified Release</td>
            <td>The verified release selected to govern runtime evaluation</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Deterministic Playbook Evaluation</td>
            <td>Evaluates normalized trusted context and returns ALLOW or DENY</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Historical or internal material may use “policy snapshot” to describe a
      compiled deterministic control representation. That phrasing is not a
      current public noun and should not replace Active Verified Release /
      Compliance Playbook hierarchy terminology.
    </p>

    <h2>Control Scopes</h2>

    <p>
      Public policy documentation separates control scopes in human-readable
      language. Do not invent public API enum values beyond verified
      implementation vocabulary.
    </p>

    <h3>Request admission controls</h3>
    <p>
      Decide whether the enterprise application may invoke an AI provider under
      the Active Verified Release. Typical concerns include trusted identity and
      tenant context, workflow identity, approved pathway status, freshness and
      replay conditions where configured, and required request attributes.
    </p>
    <p>
      DENY prevents provider invocation. ALLOW permits invocation only; it does
      not authorize later enterprise effect.
    </p>

    <h3>Result-to-action controls</h3>
    <p>
      Decide whether a declared intended enterprise action—based on an
      AI-generated result treated as an untrusted proposal—may proceed. This is
      authorization of the declared action, typically as an independent governed
      authorization where required. It is not semantic scoring of model content
      and does not resume an original AI response.
    </p>
    <p>
      DENY prevents enterprise effect. ALLOW permits the enterprise application
      to execute the authorized action.
    </p>

    <h3>Shared transaction controls</h3>
    <p>
      Shared obligations across a governed AI transaction include release
      identity binding, playbook identity, correlation of related request and
      action decisions, tenant and environment binding, and fail-closed behavior
      when required trust signals cannot be verified.
    </p>

    <h3>Evidence and verification obligations</h3>
    <p>
      Authorization decisions produce Verifiable Decision Records. Evidence
      supports investigation through BeaconGuard Verify: Decision Explorer,
      Deterministic Reconstruction, Source Evidence, Replay Verification, and
      Verifiable Export. AI-Assisted Insights are non-authoritative and must not
      enter the runtime permission path.
    </p>

    <h2>Identities</h2>

    <p>Prefer these terms in integration and evidence:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>playbook_identity</strong> — identifies the Compliance Playbook lineage</li>
      <li><strong>release_identity</strong> — identifies the Signed Immutable Release Artifact / Active Verified Release used for evaluation</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Both identities should appear in Verifiable Decision Records so decisions
      can be reconstructed against the correct release. Related decision and
      evidence identifiers support investigation of request and action
      relationships.
    </p>

    <h2>Inputs and Normalization</h2>

    <p>
      Deterministic Playbook Evaluation requires <strong>Request Validation and
      Context Normalization</strong> (and equivalent normalization for declared
      action context). Typical inputs include signed request metadata, workflow
      identity, user role, source-system trust, approved-pathway status,
      deterministic context tags, request IDs, timestamps, freshness windows,
      and—for action authorization—the declared intended enterprise action and
      required correlation context.
    </p>

    <p>Normalization ensures:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Stable field names and types</li>
      <li>Stable ordering for deterministic hashing and audit reconstruction</li>
      <li>Rejection or explicit mapping of unknown or invalid inputs</li>
      <li>No reliance on probabilistic or model-provided interpretations as authority</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Decision Outputs</h2>

    <p>Runtime permission results are binary:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ALLOW</strong> — required controls are satisfied; after evidence requirements are met, the permitted next step may proceed (provider invocation or enterprise action execution, as applicable)</li>
      <li><strong>DENY</strong> — hard stop; no provider invocation or no enterprise effect, as applicable</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      When required approval is absent, the result is <strong>DENY</strong> with a
      deterministic reason indicating that approval is required. An illustrative
      reason style is <code>APPROVAL_REQUIRED</code>. Any governance or human-review
      obligation remains separate from the runtime permission result; <strong>review
      does not permit execution</strong> and does not convert a recorded DENY into
      ALLOW.
    </p>

    <p>Outputs also include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Deterministic reasons or reason codes</li>
      <li>playbook_identity and release_identity</li>
      <li>Optional obligations (notification or review workflow only)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Runtime permission results do not include a review-as-permission state.</p>

    <h2>Provenance and Integrity</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Releases are signed before use (Signed Immutable Release Artifact)</li>
      <li>Runtime authorization accepts only an Active Verified Release</li>
      <li>release_identity is recorded on Verifiable Decision Records</li>
      <li>Rollback and activation are explicit (no silent edits to active controls)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Failure Semantics</h2>

    <p>Evaluation is <strong>fail closed</strong> where enforcement is configured.</p>

    <p>
      If required release artifacts are missing, invalid, or unverifiable, or if
      Trust, Freshness, or Replay Checks fail:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The system must return <strong>DENY</strong></li>
      <li>The Verifiable Decision Record must show the failure category and release_identity when known</li>
    </ul>

    <p>No permissive fallback is allowed.</p>

